    <item>
      <title>Debian 13.7 Released With Many Bug Fixes</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Debian-13.7-Released</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Debian-13.7-Released</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 12 Sep 2026 17:06:53 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="DEBIAN" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/debian.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; For those wanting the freshest &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search//Debian+13"&gt;Debian 13&lt;/a&gt; "Trixie" install media, Debian 13.7 is out today in bundling up all the latest bug and security fixes. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Debian 13.7 fixes with dozens of security fixes affecting the likes of the Linux kernel, Chromium, NTFS-3G, RoundCube, Samba, PHP, Postfix, Zip, Cockpit, Wireshark, and many other packages seeing security fixes. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Debian 13.7 also comes with many bug fixes such as a possible crash in Akondai Search, fixing downloads and updating of spices for Cinnamon, llvm-toolchain-22 foe supporting newer Chromium builds, and various other updates. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=debian-13-benchmarks&amp;image=debian_13_1_med" alt="Debian 13" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Downloads and more details on today's Debian 13.7 release via &lt;a href="https://www.debian.org/News/2026/20260912"&gt;Debian.org&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Debian 14 as the next major feature release of Debian Linux is expected to be out in the second half of 2027.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Void Linux Maintainer Orphans 100+ Packages Over AI Policy Dispute</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Void-Linux-AI-Policy-Orphan</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Void-Linux-AI-Policy-Orphan</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 12 Sep 2026 15:11:26 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="OPERATING SYSTEMS" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/operatingsystems.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; A Void Linux maintainer has orphaned more than 100 packages he maintained over a dispute of the project's AI policy for LLM-generated text. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Void Linux contributor Andrea Brancaleoni last week submitted &lt;a href="https://github.com/void-linux/void-packages/pull/62351"&gt;a Void package update  for Go 1.27.1&lt;/a&gt;. As a follow-up to it, yesterday was &lt;a href="https://github.com/void-linux/void-packages/pull/62351#issuecomment-5641804457"&gt;this commit&lt;/a&gt; he posted referring to the results comparing the Go 1.26 to 1.27 package upgrade impact. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Another Void Linux maintainer then asked what was used in generating this text for comparison, to which Brancaleoni noted was GLM-5.3-Flash + OpenCode. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;But the Void Linux project does have &lt;a href="https://github.com/void-linux/.github/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md#ai-usage"&gt;a contributing policy&lt;/a&gt; over AI usage that reads: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"In this section, "AI" refers to generative artificial intelligence tools, including Large Language Models and similar technologies. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;All contributions are expected to be made by humans. AI tools may be used for research and learning, but all content in contributions must originate from and be understood by the contributor. This includes code, documentation, issues, security reports, pull request descriptions, and comments in all Void Linux community spaces. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;ALL usage of AI tools MUST be disclosed. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Do not bring AI review tools into pull requests. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Usage of AI-powered human-language translation tools is acceptable to facilitate contributions across language barriers."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;So technically the policy was broken by using AI to generate comments on comments for that Void Linux pull request. And the usage of AI tools wasn't disclosed upfront in that offending message. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Now in turn, Brancaleoni decided to &lt;a href="https://github.com/void-linux/void-packages/pull/62482"&gt;disown maintained packages&lt;/a&gt; that he oversees. That has resulted in 113 of the Void Linux packages set to be orphaned. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The now orphaned packages include Adapta, Autodia, SFML, abootimg, alacritty, arpwatch, asus-kbd-backlight, backblaze-b2, bash-preexec, beep, binfmt-support, bitlbee-facebook, bitlbee-steam, cdm, clementine, crane, csound, daemonize, darkhttpd, dep, diff-pdf, docker-cli, docker-gc, docker-gen, dtc, embree, entr, etcd, fcron, flannel, flint, font-fontin, forego, freeplane, fscrypt, gede, gettext-stub, git-cola, gkill, glock, go-bindata, go-jira, hans, hivex, hopper, hostaliases, hugo, iio-sensor-proxy, indicator-doom-cpu, interbench, ispc, jbigkit, kaitai-struct-compiler, kubernetes, kvirc, libguestfs, libnfc, libpodofo0.9.7, logstalgia, massren, mbox, mfoc, mkinitcpio-nfs-utils, mksh, moby, motion, openresolv, packer, pd, perl-Template-Toolkit, pinktrace, podofo, python3-Arrow, python3-b2sdk, python3-pdm-backend, python3-xattr, racket, rakarrack, rancher-compose, rmlint, rng-tools, rocker, runit-swap, sassc, scanmem, schroot, sift, splix, sublime-text3, subtle, supermin, sydbox, tag, tbb, terraform, terragrunt, texmaker, thermald, trollock, ttf-ubuntu-font-family, urbanterror-data, urbanterror, usbredir, vagrant, vba-m, virt-manager, virtme, webcat, websocketd, wpa_actiond, xboxdrv, xcompmgr, and yoshimi. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=void_linux_disown" alt="Void Linux disown" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;That includes some notable packages such as Alacritty,  binfmt-support, Clementine, various Docker packages, fscrypt, Kubernetes, various Python 3 packages, and Intel Thermald now set to be orphaned unless other Void Linux maintainers step up to maintain them over this AI policy dispute.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Intel Reverses Decision To Cancel Their Open-Source Font Project</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-One-Mono-Saved</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-One-Mono-Saved</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 12 Sep 2026 13:45:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="INTEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/intel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Amid &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/review/intel-open-source-2025"&gt;Intel cutting back on open-source projects&lt;/a&gt; over the past two years as cost cutting measures at the company, their latest open-source project on the chopping block was the peculiar font they developed a few years back. The project was archived this week only to change course two days later. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Intel has continued &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Ends-Quantum-Intrinsics"&gt;sunsetting various open-source projects&lt;/a&gt; that are no longer actively maintained or where the Intel employees have left the organization, etc. Some of them have been a bit surprising with &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Ending-BigDL"&gt;some open-source AI/LLM projects&lt;/a&gt; being affected while in other cases no big deal like &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Archives-Optane-Ansible"&gt;open-source code for their previously-discontinued Optane memory&lt;/a&gt;. Surprisingly being archived and then reverted is their One Mono font. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Back in 2023, &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-One-Mono"&gt;Intel announced the creation of a new open-source font for developers&lt;/a&gt;. Intel One Mono is an expressive monospaced font family focusing on clarity, legibility, and "the needs of developers in mind." &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=intel_one_mono_1" alt="Intel One Mono font example" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;It was a bit surprising to see Intel develop One Mono in the first place with the CPU vendor developing an open-source font having never been on my bingo card. It hasn't seen a new release since mid-2024 and wouldn't have been surprised  at all to see it discontinued amid cost-cutting measures. But now surprisingly after it was archived this week, for whatever internal decision it's been reverted to keep the font being maintained. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=intel_one_mono_2" alt="Intel One Mono archived and unarchived" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;One Mono doesn't see much activity with the only change in 2025 being README updates and the last real code changes in July 2024. But if you happen to be a fan of this Intel font, it's still around and available via &lt;a href="https://github.com/intel/intel-one-mono"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This week they did also end up archiving &lt;a href="https://github.com/intel/Intel-AMX-Detection"&gt;Intel AMX Detection&lt;/a&gt; as a Python script for detecting Intel Advanced Matrix Extensions capabilities of a processor. The &lt;a href="https://github.com/intel/media-driver-helper"&gt;Intel Media Driver Helper&lt;/a&gt; was also discontinued as a documentation archive for their GPU accelerated media driver. On the game development side, Intel sunset their &lt;a href="https://github.com/GameTechDev/MaskedOcclusionCulling"&gt;Masked Occluson Culling code&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>GTK 4.24 Released With Better Fractional Scaling, More Precise GDK Frame-Clock</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/GTK-4.24-Released</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/GTK-4.24-Released</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 12 Sep 2026 10:52:32 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="GNOME" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/gnome.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; GTK 4.24 released on Friday as the newest stable feature release to the GTK4 toolkit. It's quite a nice update to GTK4 for pairing with next week's GNOME 51 desktop release.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;GTK 4.24 adds support for the Wayland ext-background-effect protocol that can be used for providing blurred backgrounds behind windows, such as for blurred window terminal effects. GTK 4.24 also supports Wayland's text input v3.2 protocol and other improvements.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;There is also &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/GTK-Snapping"&gt;better fractional scaling support&lt;/a&gt; with GTK 4.24 by snapping contents to device pixel boundaries. This provides for greater control of rendering when making use of fractional scaling on your device.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=gtk_424_sml" alt="GTK 4.24" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;For those preferring reduced motion behavior to avoid sensory overload or similar conditions, GTK 4.24 respects the reduced motion setting in various places and avoids animations when the  setting is enabled.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;The GDK frame-clock has been reworked to improve precision with ensuring frames are presented on-time while also reducing frame drops. GTK4 on Android is also now aligning frame timing with vsyncs.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;GTK's scalable vector graphics (SVG) support has also been greatly improved with the GTK 4.24 toolkit.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Beyond that feature work, GTK 4.24 also brings a lot of bug fixes. Quite a lot.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Sadly, the session save/restore functionality was diverted to GTK 4.26. GTK developers had been &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/GTK-FOSDEM-2026-Hackfest"&gt;planning nice session saving support&lt;/a&gt; but it didn't make the cut for GTK 4.24.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;GTK 4.24 can be downloaded from &lt;a href="https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/gtk/-/tags/4.24.0"&gt;GNOME's GitLab&lt;/a&gt; for those wanting to build the latest toolkit ahead of next week's official GNOME 51 release.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Intel Linux NPU Driver Only Now Officially Supports Ubuntu 26.04 LTS</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Linux-NPU-Driver-1.38</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Linux-NPU-Driver-1.38</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 12 Sep 2026 10:34:25 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="INTEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/intel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Releasing on Friday was the Intel Linux NPU Driver 1.38 as the user-space open-source driver components for leveraging the Core Ultra Neural Processing Unit (NPU) under Linux in conjunction with the upstream IVPU kernel accelerator driver.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;With the new Intel Linux NPU Driver 1.38 release there is just one listed change and a rather surprising one at that... official support for Ubuntu 26.04 LTS.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=intel_npu_138_1_med" alt="Intel NPU Linux stack overview" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;The Intel NPU Linux stack has worked on Ubuntu 26.04 LTS already, but only now is officially recognized by Intel and formally supported by their packaged user-space driver stack. That's five months after Ubuntu 26.04 debuted and right ahead of the upcoming Ubuntu 26.10 release.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=intel_npu_138_2" alt="Intel NPU Linux 1.38 driver release" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Meteor Lake, Arrow Lake, Lunar Lake, Panther  Lake, and Wildcat Lake have all been tested now on Ubuntu 26.04 LTS to ensure the user-space NPU driver is working properly. This is done on Ubuntu 26.04 LTS with the default Linux 7.0 kernel build. Ubuntu 24.04 LTS continues to be officially supported too by this driver stack.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Aside from officially supporting Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, there are no other listed changes with the new Intel NPU driver 1.38 release. Those interested can download the new user mode driver on &lt;a href="https://github.com/intel/linux-npu-driver/releases/tag/v1.38.0"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Ubuntu Developers Contemplate Linux Firmware HWE Package</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Ubuntu-Considers-Linux-Firm-HWE</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Ubuntu-Considers-Linux-Firm-HWE</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 12 Sep 2026 10:25:11 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="UBUNTU" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/ubuntu.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; A possible future enhancement being considered for Ubuntu Long Term Support "LTS" releases is the introduction of a &lt;em&gt;linux-firmware-hwe&lt;/em&gt; package as part of their hardware enablement (HWE) stack. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Ubuntu currently ships updated &lt;em&gt;linux-firmware&lt;/em&gt; packages as stable release updates, but it can end up being a bit problematic over time where hardware vendors aren't typically testing newer firmware binaries against older kernel versions and the like, which can lead to regressions. The idea raised this week is having a &lt;em&gt;linux-firmware-hwe&lt;/em&gt; package tied to the Linux HWE kernels on Ubuntu LTS versions to better align the kernel and firmware. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=system76-thelio-major-9980x&amp;image=thelio_major_4_med" alt="Modern desktop hardware" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Canonical engineer Juerg Haefliger elaborated on the Ubuntu mailing list: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"The problem at the moment is that backporting firmware to LTS releases for their respective HWE kernels often negatively impacts users running the GA kernel. Old kernel with new firmware is not a combination that firmware vendors typically test. Firmware updates between Ubuntu releases have grown significantly in recent years, resulting in larger backports and a higher chance of regressions. This approach to firmware backports has long been a thorn in the side of the SRU team, and rightly so. Just the recent backports to Noble for the 7.0 HWE kernel introduced at least two AMD regressions for 6.9 kernel users :-( &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;To address this, I’d like to propose introducing linux-firmware-hwe (along with HWE variants for all subpackages). HWE kernels would depend on linux-firmware-hwe, which in turn would provide and break linux-firmware so only one set of packages can be installed at a time. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This will prevent regressions for GA-only users. For systems that have both GA and HWE kernels installed, all kernels will load the HWE firmwares, meaning users might still encounter firmware-related issues when booting the GA kernel."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The change is still being considered and if it goes ahead it could end up happening for Ubuntu 26.04 LTS still. The linux-firmware-hwe package may end up being introduced in Ubuntu 26.10 so that when the HWE stack for Ubuntu 26.04 LTS pulls in the 26.10 components, this firmware HWE package would be introduced. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=ubuntu-2104-epyc&amp;image=ubuntu_2104_epyc1_med" alt="Ubuntu server" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;We'll see how &lt;a href="https://lists.ubuntu.com/archives/ubuntu-devel/2026-September/043647.html"&gt;this proposal&lt;/a&gt; plays out but it makes sense for enhancing the Ubuntu Linux LTS HWE stack for ensuring new firmware for hardware enablment focused users while reducing the regression risk for those sticking to the original GA kernel versions.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>KDE Plasma 6.8 Remote Desktop To Enjoy Lower Latency Performance</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.8-KRDP-Lower-Lat</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.8-KRDP-Lower-Lat</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 12 Sep 2026 10:07:48 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="KDE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/kde.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Along with &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.8-Beta"&gt;KDE Plasma 6.8 beta releasing this week&lt;/a&gt;, there were some other interesting Plasma changes this week worthy of a shout-out. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For those using remote desktop capabilities with KRDP, remote desktop connections should now enjoy lower latency performance. The change made by David Edmundson is to drop incoming frames when the queue builds up for when the encoder is running quicker than the client can encode. The change is described as "&lt;em&gt;keeps latency very fast.&lt;/em&gt;" &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=nvidia-595-kde-gnome&amp;image=nvidia_kde_2_med" alt="KDE Plasma desktop" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Squeezing in for Plasma 6.8 are improvements to the Input Method widget for enhancing the experience of switching between on-screen keyboard visibility roles as well as for manually showing the keyboard in XWayland apps. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Clipboard settings around copied image data and MIME actions are now more comprehensible in time for the Plasma 6.8 release. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Also landing this week was &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-KWin-Wayland-Commit-Timing"&gt;Wayland commit-timing protocol support for KWin&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Plasma 6.8 will also no longer crash if querying the wallpaper using D-Bus while the wallpaper settings dialog is open and then switching between wallpaper plug-ins. Invalid configuration files within &lt;em&gt;/etc/xdg/Xwayland-session.d/&lt;/em&gt; will also no longer prevent KWin from launching KWin. Plus various other fixes throughout. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on the many last minute Plasma changes this week can be found via &lt;a href="https://blogs.kde.org/2026/09/12/this-week-in-plasma-6.8-beta-release/"&gt;This Week in Plasma&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Plasma 6.8 Beta 2 is coming up on 24 September while the official release of Plasma 6.8.0 is expected on 14 October that also marks the 30th anniversary of KDE's inception.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Gitte As Git Client For GNOME Continues Maturing Quite Nicely</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Gitte-0.10-Released</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Gitte-0.10-Released</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 12 Sep 2026 00:35:39 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="GNOME" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/gnome.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; For those in pursuit of a graphical Git client for GNOME or the Linux desktop at large, the GTK4-powered Gitte continues advancing rather well and out this week is its v0.10 release. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Gitte 0.10 adds the ability to show side-by-side diffs in its GTK4 UI, Git LFS and submodules support, new shortcuts for enhancing the workflow, and various UI improvements too. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=gitte_1" alt="Gitte side-by-side diff" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Plus Gitte 0.10 enjoys bug fixes and more as outlined in the latest issue of &lt;a href="https://thisweek.gnome.org/posts/2026/09/twig-265/"&gt;This Week in GNOME&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=gitte_2" alt="Gitte UI" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;GNOME this week also saw &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-51-Custom-Accel-Profiles"&gt;custom pointer acceleration profiles for GNOME 51&lt;/a&gt;, many recent improvements to the GNOME Calendar app, and other work. GNOME 51 meanwhile is set for release next week on 16 September.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>New Cache Aware Scheduling Fixes Out For Testing</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Cache-Aware-Scheduling-4-Patch</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Cache-Aware-Scheduling-4-Patch</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 11 Sep 2026 18:00:14 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX KERNEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxkernel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Since being merged back during the Linux 7.2 cycle, the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Cache+Aware+Scheduling"&gt;Cache Aware Scheduling&lt;/a&gt; code continues seeing more fixes as users test out this scheduling enhancement on different processors and system configurations. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Following &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-rc2-Scheduler-Fixes"&gt;last week's Cache Aware Scheduling fix for Intel hybrid CPUs&lt;/a&gt;, more material is available for testing this week for enhancing the code. As a reminder, Cache Aware Scheduling is the Linux kernel feature for grouping related tasks together on CPU cores that share the same last level cache (LLC). Cache Aware Scheduling can lead to better cache locality and lower latency by avoiding the costs of potential cache bouncing.  &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;These new fixes deal with tasks potentially being stranded from the outside or yanked away from their preferred last level cache. There is also a use-after-free fix too for the scheduling code. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2023&amp;image=intel_microcode_valentine_med" alt="Intel CPUs" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Intel engineer Tim Chen who has been leading the charge on Cache Aware Scheduling for the Linux kernel sent out &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/cover.1789061845.git.tim.c.chen@linux.intel.com/"&gt;this patch series&lt;/a&gt; collecting those recent fixes. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Not addressed by this patch series, but there are also known issues with Cache Aware Scheduling interfering with Intel Turbo Boost Max Technology (ITMT). That work isn't addressed by these patches but hopefully some new improvements there will surface soon.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Linux's IO_uring Sees Some "Crazy" Patches That Deliver Very Nice Improvements</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-IO-uring-Crazy-Friday</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-IO-uring-Crazy-Friday</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 11 Sep 2026 16:21:33 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX STORAGE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxstorage.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Linux block subsystem maintainer and IO_uring lead developer &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Jens+Axboe"&gt;Jens Axboe&lt;/a&gt; continues to uncover new opportunities for making the wonderful IO_uring framework even more performant within the Linux kernel. For ending out the week he's posted some "crazy"  patches delivering some significant gains. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Axboe's latest work is focused on improvving the kernel blocking behavior. Axboe described the work in today's &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/io-uring/20260911154148.644489-1-axboe@kernel.dk/"&gt;RFC patch series&lt;/a&gt;: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"io_uring issues requests inline with IO_URING_F_NONBLOCK and punts to io-wq when that isn't possible. For a range of opcodes it isn't possible at all, as there's no nonblocking path in the kernel for them: fsync, statx, openat, the *at family, xattr, fadvise, splice, etc. Those are punted unconditionally, and the punt costs a thread wakeup, a context switch and a task_work completion round trip per request. io_uring HAS to be cautious to prevent accidental blocking in the kernel, even if the operations predominantly never block. Sad story. Examples of that are things like an fdatasync that doesn't block, statx that hits dcache, openat for O_TMPFILE, etc. All of those would've completed inline just fine, but io_uring just cannot rely on that. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;This series issues those requests inline in blocking mode instead, and only pays for the offload if the request actually blocks. But by the time it blocks, the submitter is deep in the kernel with the request on its stack, so the work can't be moved to another thread. What we can move is the identity. If the submitting task blocks, an idle io-wq worker takes over its user visible identity (tid, signal state, credentials, scheduling attributes, cgroup, user register state), finishes the io_uring_enter() call and returns to userspace as the submitter. The original task finishes the request as an io-wq worker and joins the pool. Userspace is none the wiser, hopefully, the same tid came back from the syscall, it's just on a different task_struct. Folks that have been around a while may remember earlier attempts at this about 20 years ago."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Axboe posted on &lt;a href="https://x.com/axboe/status/2098437408127676710"&gt;X&lt;/a&gt; about these "crazy patches" and in turn shared some graphics of these new "tasty results" out of IO_uring, which will be most exciting for users not concerned about the internal technicals of this change: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=axboe_crazy_iouring_1" alt="IO_uring benchmark results" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=axboe_crazy_iouring_2" alt="IO_uring benchmark results" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;We'll see where this work leads and if it ends up being ready for mainline by the Linux v7.4 cycle.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
